Work queue (in this order, per Andres - new items get appended at the end unless he
says otherwise). Reordered 2026-08-08 by gain/effort/surface-area/code-practice -
see the note above each moved/merged item for what changed and why.

Everything below is the general backlog (not yet in a decided order):

Bigger features needing research first (moderate-high surface area):

- Open-source prep (Andres 2026-08-24: mid-term priority, after the
  current architecture-cleanup pass): read through Claude Code's own docs (and blogs) end to
  end, looking for features/patterns worth adopting here (this session
  already found one - statusLine JSON carries rate_limits.* directly, see
  the quota-capture work in progress). Once that pass is done:
  - Write up end-user docs, a CONTRIBUTING guide (install scripts,
    requirements, setup steps), and treat the README as a portfolio piece.
    Call out explicitly: this project deliberately avoids Anthropic
    cloud-side features/dependencies - everything runs locally on the
    same machine the site runs on (LAN-only, no external relay, no
    account/login of its own), EXCEPT for the actual model responses
    themselves and however the `claude` CLI process/its own API calls
    inherently work, which are Anthropic's, not this app's.
  - Audit for unnecessary fallbacks and remove any found.
  - Remove any remaining hardcoded values or assumptions tied to Andres's
    own laptop/personal workflow (paths, usernames, machine-specific
    conventions) - most of this was already done ahead of open-sourcing
    (see Config.php's own docblock, dated 2026-08-09), but re-check with
    fresh eyes before actually opening the repo up.
  - Then open the repo to outside users/contributors.

- Persistent "pending answers" list: track every prompt/permission that
  hasn't been answered yet (either a small state file this app maintains
  itself, or derived by reading all sessions from Claude's own global
  ~/.claude/projects path), surviving session archival, removed once
  answered - shown on the dashboard so waking the laptop and opening the
  app immediately shows what's actually waiting, without hunting through
  each session.
- Mode CHANGES (accept edits / plan / manual / auto) aren't currently
  visible as discrete events in the transcript at all -
  render_mode_toggle_html() only shows the CURRENT mode, there's no
  record of "mode switched from X to Y at this point." (Split off from
  the plan-recognition item, shipped 2026-08-07 - see TranscriptService::
  find_exit_plan_mode_tool_use_ids()/parse_transcript_line() for that
  half.) Research already done: the transcript JSONL DOES have a
  dedicated line for this - {"type":"permission-mode","permissionMode":
  "default"|"acceptEdits"|"plan"|"auto"} (default=manual in this app's
  own terminology) - but it's logged redundantly (239 occurrences in one
  real session, mostly repeats of the unchanged value, not one-per-real-
  transition) and has NO timestamp field. Surfacing genuine "switched
  from X to Y here" events needs tracking last-seen mode across
  consecutive lines while scanning (stateful), which doesn't fit
  parse_transcript_line()'s current per-line-stateless model - and is
  harder for backward pagination ("Load older messages") than forward
  polling, since "previous" flips meaning depending on walk direction.
  Worth deciding: full bidirectional support, or a smaller scope (e.g.
  only ever detected going forward from whenever a session was first
  opened, never backfilled into older history)?

- Wire up StatuslineMarkerService::install_statusline_marker()/
  check_statusline_marker() into a dispatch_action() case - unlike
  install_session_hook's own check_session_hook/install_session_hook
  pair, there's currently no way to actually install the statusline
  marker from the running app. Confirmed 2026-08-22 (full codebase audit)
  as the only dead-code instance in the repo - the audit itself also
  found and fixed a resume_cc_session() race condition and two PHP/JS
  mirror-pair drifts, all already shipped.

Larger architecture / consolidation work (bigger surface area):

- IN PROGRESS (started 2026-08-24, superseding the earlier "long-term, not
  near-term" sequencing below - Andres asked to start directly): support
  Google's Antigravity CLI (`agy`) as a second agent alongside Claude Code,
  via a real AgentAdapter interface. Full research findings, phased plan,
  and an up-to-date "Status at a glance" section (read this first - it's
  kept current, most recently 2026-08-24) live in
  docs/antigravity-adapter-plan.md - read that before touching anything
  here, including a coordination note since Andres runs Claude and his
  own `agy` session against this same repo (not simultaneously, but
  independently). Spawn/identity/hooks/transcript-rendering/quota-polling
  are done and verified live; agent-name + per-agent quota DISPLAY is
  also done (built by Andres's own `agy` session, finished/reviewed/
  committed by Claude after it hit its account quota mid-task). Still
  open: permission-mode display, blocked-prompt detection (the harder
  one - needs pane-text parsing), reading this app's own CLAUDE.md-style
  conventions into Antigravity sessions somehow, and verifying whether
  plain assistant text messages render correctly (flagged by Andres,
  status unconfirmed either way as of the last update to the plan doc).
- Multi-socket tmux discovery: today the whole app is wired to exactly one
  tmux socket path (Config::tmux_socket(), defaulting to
  /tmp/tmux-<uid>/default - tmux's own standard default path, not an app
  convention, which is why a plain `tmux` command Andres types by hand
  lands on the same server the app manages). Every TmuxService call is
  scoped to that one path - if a session ends up on a DIFFERENT tmux
  server (a different explicit -S path, or a fresh server auto-spawned at
  the conventional path after the original one's socket became
  unreachable - see the 2026-08-08 incident where /tmp/tmux-1000's
  contents got deleted out from under a still-running server, orphaning
  its sessions with no clean recovery path found), the app has zero
  visibility into it. Fixing this properly needs: TmuxService enumerating
  every tmux socket under /tmp/tmux-$UID/ (not just "default") and
  querying each one, SidecarStore gaining a field recording which socket a
  session actually lives on, and the SessionStart hook recording that at
  adoption time (today it only records the session NAME via `tmux
  display-message -p '#S'`, not which socket it ran that query against).
  A real structural change, not a small addition - scope/design it
  properly before starting, don't bolt it onto an unrelated phase. (The
  related "a live-but-untracked session shows up in the archived list
  too" gap this used to note is resolved now that Resume and Take over
  both ship - see the unify-claude-sessions plan, phases 5-6 shipped
  2026-08-08 - either action works correctly regardless of which
  category a row actually falls into.)
Smaller polish (low effort, low risk):

- Bug report (Andres 2026-08-24), not yet investigated: quota-reset push
  notifications now fire at the right time, but Andres gets TWO for the
  same reset - one correct (reset), one with stale data still claiming
  100% used. Possible race between whatever marks the reset as sent and
  whatever reads the last-known quota state to build the push body -
  check PushDeliveryService::check_and_send_quota_pushes() and
  QuotaService's live-state file read/write ordering for a window where a
  second push can be built/sent off the pre-reset value after the first
  (correct) one already went out.
- Bug report, not yet investigated (Andres 2026-08-23): "maybe some
  entries are missing after reloading a session" - session.php. Unclear
  yet whether this means transcript entries specifically, or something
  else (sidebar Tasks, blocked-prompt state); no repro steps captured
  yet. Needs a proper look - check TranscriptService::read_transcript_page()/
  the initial-load vs poll-time entry set, and whether a hard page reload
  (not a poll) takes a different code path that could drop something a
  poll wouldn't.
- session.php: add keyboard shortcuts to navigate between sessions -
  left/right to move to the prev/next session, plus a popup to search/jump
  by name.
- Bug report (Andres 2026-08-24), with a live mobile screenshot: tool-call
  outputs sometimes get attached/"staggered" onto the wrong call. In the
  screenshot (WS2818 LED strip session), a "Bash: pwd && ls -la" entry is
  immediately followed by an "ssh rpi 'hostname; uname -a; cat
  /etc/os-release...'" tool-call entry whose own collapsed result shows
  "/home/user/dev/Rpi/Ws2818 (empty)" - that's the PREVIOUS pwd/ls
  call's output, not the ssh command's - while the ssh command's real
  output (a post-quantum warning + real hostname/uname/os-release text)
  renders as its own separate, un-paired entry below instead of nested
  inside the ssh tool-call entry. Looks like a tool_use/tool_result
  pairing bug, likely in renderEntriesGrouped()/entryIsGroupableToolCall()
  (session.js) or its PHP mirror (TranscriptView) - back-to-back Bash
  calls arriving close together (e.g. during live polling) may be
  mismatching which result attaches to which call. Needs a live repro
  with the actual transcript JSONL lines to see the real call/result
  ordering before diagnosing further.
